  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 113 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nAfter making a brief jaunt towards Arizona and southern California, \nsubtropical ridging will bounce back and extend east over our area \nthis week, encouraging steady southerly flow and the return of \nhigher dew points. Afternoon highs may moderate some into the mid \nand upper 90s, but the added moisture is expected to boost heat \nindices back above 105 for the I-35 corridor, Coastal Plains, and \nRio Grande Plains mid to late week. This is climatologically the \nhottest time of the year, and the heat can become hazardous if \nadequate steps are not taken to stay cool and hydrated. Overnight \nlows will stick around the mid to upper 70s.\n\nThe southerly flow should restore the typical cycle of morning \ncloudiness breaking into mostly sunny afternoons, but the overhead \nridge should keep the weather rain free for most of South Central \nTexas through the week. Later in the week, a broad trough swings \nacross the northern tier of CONUS, which results in a slight \nweakness in the ridging. While there are no clear cut disturbances \nin the forecast at this time, this slight weakness could provide a \nslight increase in isolated seabreeze-related shower activity mainly \nThursday onwards. These low rain chances (around 20 percent at best) \nmainly involve the Coastal Plains, but if the weakness opens up \nfurther, a few showers could push farther inland.",
